\begin{align*}
x^8 + 2x^6 - 4x^4 + 8x^2 + 16 &= (x^8 + 4x^6 + 4x^4) - (4x^4 + 8x^2 + 16) \\
&= (x^4 + 2x^2 + 4)^2 - 20(x^2 + 2)^2 + 40 \\
&= (x^4 + 2x^2 - 2x\sqrt{5} + 4)(x^4 + 2x^2 + 2x\sqrt{5} + 4) - 20(x^2 + 2)^2 + 40.
\end{align*}
Since $(x^4 + 2x^2 - 2x\sqrt{5} + 4)$ and $(x^4 + 2x^2 + 2x\sqrt{5} + 4)$ are both positive for all real $x,$ the denominator is always positive. Moreover, the maximum value of the expression occurs when the denominator is minimized. To find the minimum value of the denominator, we apply AM-GM inequality:
\begin{align*}
(x^4 + 2x^2 - 2x\sqrt{5} + 4)(x^4 + 2x^2 + 2x\sqrt{5} + 4) &\geq \left(\sqrt{(x^4 + 2x^2 - 2x\sqrt{5} + 4)(x^4 + 2x^2 + 2x\sqrt{5} + 4)}\right)^2 \\
&\geq \left(\sqrt{(x^4 + 2x^2)^2 - (2x\sqrt{5})^2 + 16}\right)^2 \\
&= (x^4 + 2x^2 + 4)^2.
\end{align*}
Equality occurs when $x^4 + 2x^2 - 2x\sqrt{5} + 4 = x^4 + 2x^2 + 2x\sqrt{5} + 4,$ i.e., $x = \pm \sqrt{5}.$ Therefore, the minimum value of the denominator is $(x^4 + 2x^2 + 4)^2 = (5 + 10 + 4)^2 = 29^2 = \boxed{841},$ and the maximum value of the expression is $\boxed{\frac{1}{29}}.$ </think> <answer>$\frac{1}{29}$</answer>

